# Abstract continuation follows the format's two-page abstract placement.
abstract_style=ParagraphStyle(name="AbstractContinuation",fontName="Times-Roman",fontSize=10.8,leading=15.3,alignment=TA_JUSTIFY,spaceAfter=9)
abstract_title=ParagraphStyle(name="AbstractContinuationTitle",fontName="Times-Bold",fontSize=14,leading=18,alignment=TA_CENTER,spaceAfter=14)
abstract_page_one=[
"SmartMail Assistant is an AI-assisted application for preparing draft replies to existing email messages. The project addresses the routine effort involved in writing acknowledgements, confirmations, clarifications, and short updates. It accepts the text of an incoming message and returns candidate wording that the user can review, revise, and copy or place into a draft. The intended outcome is reduced repetitive drafting effort while keeping the sender responsible for accuracy, tone, and the final decision to communicate.",
"The prototype combines a React and Material UI web interface, a Spring Boot REST service, Google's Gemini generative AI API, and a Chrome Manifest V3 content script intended for Gmail. In the web path, a user enters email content, may select Professional, Casual, or Friendly tone, and activates Generate Reply. The interface checks for blank input, shows a loading state while waiting, displays returned text, and provides a copy action. In the Gmail path, the content script observes page changes, attempts to add an AI Reply control, reads visible message text, requests a reply from the same backend route, and attempts to insert returned text into the compose editor.",
"The backend provides a common model-access boundary for both clients. A request containing emailContent and tone is bound by the REST controller and passed to a service. The service constructs a prompt, sends a contents-and-parts request to the configured Gemini endpoint, parses the first candidate text, and returns a string to the client. The system therefore demonstrates an end-to-end architecture with separate presentation, API, model-integration, and browser-integration responsibilities. Its current request and response contract remains compact and needs further definition for production use.",
"The product is designed as a drafting assistant rather than an automatic email sender. Generated text can be fluent yet incomplete or inaccurate, so the user must verify names, dates, amounts, factual statements, and commitments before using it. The project source does not establish account management, persistent reply history, analytics, attachment processing, or automatic sending. Email content is transmitted through the backend to an external model provider, making data handling and user notice part of the project context."
]
abstract_page_two=[
"Source review indicates that the main web workflow, REST route, Gemini request, and Gmail action are represented in code. The implementation is nevertheless a prototype with integration and operational limits. The React page and Gmail script are separate clients; the extension uses a fixed tone and local endpoint; Gmail interaction depends on page selectors; server-side validation is limited; and the provider-response parser assumes a first candidate and first text part. The controller returns raw text and does not show a complete typed error contract for invalid input, provider failure, or malformed output. The configured local HTTP endpoint is intended for development rather than public deployment.",
"This report distinguishes observed source behavior from recommendations and planned verification. No executed end-to-end test log, live Gemini output, benchmark, user study, or production deployment record was supplied as project evidence. The included Work 12 screenshot shows the web interface before input, with the generate action disabled. Work 13 shows a Gmail compose view with the AI Reply control. These images demonstrate visible interface states only; they do not prove that a provider response was generated or inserted into a draft.",
"A complete evaluation should use synthetic or consented email examples and record the source revision, runtime versions, browser, configuration category, observed result, and evidence reference. Deterministic tests can mock the provider to check prompt construction and parsing. Separate integration checks can cover network configuration, timeout behavior, and provider errors. Browser checks should cover loading and recovery states, keyboard access, copy behavior, multiple compose windows, message extraction, draft preservation, and protection against unintended sending. Security review should examine secret handling, request-size limits, CORS, HTTPS, diagnostic logs, and the privacy notice against the actual deployment.",
"The project contributes a working architectural starting point for integrating generative text assistance into email drafting through both a web page and a Gmail-oriented browser action. The next priorities are to validate fields and tone values on the server, align the base prompt with selected tones, handle empty and blocked model responses, configure endpoints and credentials securely, and make Gmail insertion resilient and accessible. The user should always retain control of the final message. The source supports the feasibility of the proposed workflow, while measured claims about reliability, productivity, and privacy require further implementation and evidence."
]
